WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
As surface temperature rises, the peak of a thermal

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.680
spectrum shifts toward shorter wavelengths. A hotter star emits

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.380
more blue light relative to visible light. Watch the

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.720
spectral curve change, not just its label. The lecture

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.384
compares the visual to blue brightness ratio: about one

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:19.824
point eight seven for the Sun and zero point

00:00:19.824 --> 00:00:23.140
eight one for Bellatrix. The hotter star has the

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.560
smaller visual to blue ratio. Now keep the star

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.560
hot but put dust in the line of sight.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.460
Dust preferentially removes blue light from the direct beam.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.460
A red observed color can mean a cooler star

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.680
or a reddened hotter star; color alone does not

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.816
resolve that ambiguity.
